external:        The categorization of the data hosted, and therefore the required
                 algorithm strength, is an organizational determination recorded in the
                 system security plan.
external:        FIPS mode depends on the host operating system providing a validated
                 cryptographic module and, per splunk-launch.conf.spec:114-115, a kernel
                 operating in FIPS mode; the operating system baseline carries that part.
external:        SPLUNK_FIPS is a one-time setting that must be in place before the
                 instance first starts (splunk-launch.conf.spec:118-122), so remediation of
                 this condition is carried by the build or installation procedure rather
                 than by a configuration change on a running instance.

citation:        CHANGED for INVENTION and a verdict-altering false finding in the
                 sslVersions test. (1) The reviewed regex
                 ^\s*(tls1\.2|\*\s*,\s*-ssl3\s*,\s*-tls1\.0\s*,\s*-tls1\.1)\s*$ admits
                 exactly two spellings and rejects hardened values the specification
                 explicitly permits. Tested against the reviewed pattern, all three of
                 'tls1.2,-ssl3', '*,-ssl2,-ssl3,-tls1.0,-tls1.1' and
                 'tls,-ssl3,-tls1.0,-tls1.1' fail, yet web.conf.spec:245 and
                 server.conf.spec:772-773 state that '-ssl2 is accepted in the version list
                 but does nothing', and :242-244 and :769 make 'tls' a legitimate positive
                 term. An instance hardened with the -ssl2 spelling, which is the form the
                 vendor's own default files have historically carried, would be written up
                 as a finding with no weak protocol enabled anywhere. The replacement is a
                 two-condition property test, both halves anchored so it behaves
                 identically under re.search and re.fullmatch: the first restricts the
                 list's positive term and confines the rest to exclusions, the second
                 requires all three weak versions to be excluded whenever the positive term
                 is a wildcard. Verified over 18 values: it passes tls1.2, tls1.2 with
                 exclusions, '*,-ssl3,-tls1.0,-tls1.1' in both spaced and unspaced form,
                 '*,-ssl2,-ssl3,-tls1.0,-tls1.1' and 'tls,-ssl3,-tls1.0,-tls1.1'; it fails
                 '*', 'tls', '*,-ssl2', '*,-ssl3,-tls1.0', 'tls1.1', 'ssl3',
                 'tls1.0,tls1.2', 'tls1.2,tls1.1' and the empty value. Note for
                 maintenance, not a defect: the spec set shipped here lists only ssl3,
                 tls1.0, tls1.1 and tls1.2 (web.conf.spec:241, server.conf.spec:767), so a
                 value of tls1.3 fails both patterns; if a later maintenance release adds
                 tls1.3 to the vocabulary the first pattern must be widened, or this
                 criterion will fail a stronger configuration. (2) SPLUNK_FIPS equals:1 is
                 outside the vocabulary the authority itself uses:
                 splunk-launch.conf.spec:110 declares [0|1] but :115 and :119-120 instruct
                 the operator to set it to 'true', so an instance configured exactly as the
                 specification directs would fail. Relaxed to in:1|true. (3) No scope
                 change: every condition remains a Splunk configuration value, and the
                 kernel and module dependency plus the one-time install constraint are
                 stated in external_dependencies so remediation is not misrepresented as a
                 live configuration edit.
finding:         Splunk Enterprise is not configured to restrict the Splunk Web and splunkd
                 listeners to TLS 1.2 with FIPS mode enabled, so the application is not
                 configured to the extent the product allows toward approved encryption
                 strength for remote connections.
